#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Compositional code-signal vocabulary for bridge_plan.code_signals().

Fifty-nine signals across ten families, each verified by running its regex
against purpose-written fixtures and then adversarially refuted: well over a
hundred were proposed and the majority dropped for matching prose, matching
nothing, or implying something that did not follow. (Count is authoritative
via `len(SIGNALS)`; this prose is a description, not the source of truth.)

The point is COMPOSITION, not classification. There is no table of program
archetypes here and there must never be one: real programs combine concerns in
more ways than anyone can enumerate, and the nearest-looking archetype is
routinely the wrong answer. Each signal is independent evidence carrying its
own implication; the agent takes the union of what fired, minus what
argues_against rules out.

argues_against is the half that keeps plans small. A vocabulary that only ever
adds emitters pushes every agent toward the blanket selection this project
refuses, so a signal that rules something out earns its place as much as one
that argues for.

Every regex is applied with re.MULTILINE | re.IGNORECASE to raw file text.
There is no parsing, so a docstring or a dependency list that merely NAMES an
API scores a hit. That is why each signal carries false_positive_traps, and why
a count of 1 in a docs-shaped file should be read as noise.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

